<Base title={"About Embodied Music Pedagogy | Melissa Bremmer & Luc Nijs"} description={"A music educational framework combining dynamical systems theory and the constraints-led approach with embodied interaction, developed by Melissa Bremmer and Luc Nijs."} path="/about" heading={"About Embodied Music Pedagogy"}>
  <div class="col about page-end">
    <h6 class="head22 blue">Embodied Music Pedagogy</h6>
    <div class="intro">
      <p>Embodied Music Pedagogy is a music educational framework, developed by Melissa Bremmer and Luc Nijs.</p>
      <p>&nbsp;</p>
      <p>Embodied Music Pedagogy builds on state-of-the-art research-based theories, developed in domains such as musicology, educational sciences, psychology, and sports sciences.</p>
      <p>&nbsp;</p>
      <p>The theoretical core of Embodied Music Pedagogy is the combination of dynamical systems theory and the constraints-led approach with the theory of embodied interaction.</p>
      <p>&nbsp;</p>
      <p>The heart of Embodied Music Pedagogy is the practice of music learning and teaching.</p>
    </div>
    <h6 class="head22 blue people-head">People</h6>
    <div class="person">
      <img src="/images/melissa-bremmer.png" alt="Melissa Bremmer - Embodied Music Pedagogy" />
      <div>
        <p><span class="heavy">Melissa Bremmer</span> is Professor Arts Education at the Amsterdam University of the Arts, the Netherlands. She holds a bachelor’s degree in music education from the Conservatorium van Amsterdam, a master’s degree in educational sciences from the University of Amsterdam, and a PhD from the University of Exeter. Her research focuses on Embodied Music Pedagogy, inclusion in music education and ArtsSciences-education. She is a member of the Music in Special Education and Music Therapy Commission (ISME).</p>
        <a class="small-link" href="https://www.ahk.nl/lectoraten/educatie/onderzoeksgroep/melissa-bremmer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">webpage</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="person">
      <img src="/images/luc-nijs.png" alt="Luc Nijs - Embodied Music Pedagogy" />
      <div>
        <p><span class="heavy">Luc Nijs</span> is Professor in Early Childhood Music Education and course director of the Bachelor in Music Education at the University of Luxembourg, and Visiting professor at the University of Ghent, affiliated to IPEM and to the Jonet Chair on Social Action and Music Making. He holds master’s degrees in music (Brussels) and philosophy (Ghent), and a PhD in systematic Musicology (Ghent). His work focuses on the role of the body in instrumental music learning, on music educational technology, and creativity.</p>
        <a class="small-link" href="https://www.uni.lu/fhse-en/people/luc-nijs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">webpage</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
